    numericals: [
      { id: 1, difficulty: "Easy", question: "An error of omission means:", options: ["A transaction left out of the books, wholly or partly", "A transaction recorded at the wrong amount", "A capital item treated as revenue", "Two errors that cancel each other"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Omission is about something missing — the whole transaction, or one side of its posting." },
      { id: 2, difficulty: "Easy", question: "Machinery purchased for ₹50,000 was debited to Purchases A/c. This is:", options: ["An error of principle", "An error of commission", "An error of omission", "A compensating error"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "A capital expenditure charged to a revenue account is the textbook error of principle." },
      { id: 3, difficulty: "Easy", question: "A complete omission affects the Trial Balance:", options: ["Not at all — both sides are equally missing the transaction", "On the debit side only", "On the credit side only", "It always makes it disagree by the full amount"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "A transaction missing from BOTH sides leaves the totals untouched — the Trial Balance still agrees." },
      { id: 4, difficulty: "Easy", question: "The Suspense A/c is opened:", options: ["Only when the Trial Balance disagrees", "For every error found", "Only at the start of the year", "Only for errors of principle"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "The Suspense A/c exists solely to carry the Trial Balance difference until the one-sided error(s) causing it are found." },
      { id: 5, difficulty: "Easy", question: "Rectifying entries are recorded in the:", options: ["Journal Proper", "Cash Book", "Purchases Book", "Trial Balance"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "The Journal Proper is the book for entries that fit no subsidiary book — including all rectifications." },
      { id: 6, difficulty: "Easy", question: "A Trial Balance that agrees proves that:", options: ["Only that equal debits and credits were recorded — NOT that the books are error-free", "That every transaction is recorded correctly", "That no error of any kind exists", "That the arithmetic of every ledger account is correct"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Omission, principle and compensating errors all leave the Trial Balance agreeing — agreement proves much less than students assume." },
      { id: 7, difficulty: "Medium", question: "₹3,000 paid to Ram was debited to Shyam's account. Rectifying entry:", options: ["Ram's A/c Dr. ₹3,000; To Shyam's A/c ₹3,000", "Shyam's A/c Dr. ₹3,000; To Ram's A/c ₹3,000", "Suspense A/c Dr. ₹3,000; To Ram's A/c ₹3,000", "Cash A/c Dr. ₹3,000; To Ram's A/c ₹3,000"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Wrong account of the same class: debit the right person (Ram received the money) and credit the wrong one (Shyam). The Trial Balance agreed, so no Suspense is involved." },
      { id: 8, difficulty: "Medium", question: "A credit sale of ₹2,400 to Kiran was posted as ₹2,400 to Kiran's account but the Sales account was never credited. The Trial Balance:", options: ["Disagrees — the credit side is short by ₹2,400", "Agrees — the debit exists", "Agrees — both entries were in the Journal", "Disagrees by ₹4,800"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "This is a partial omission — the Journal entry was right, but one side (Sales) was never posted to the Ledger, so the credit total falls short by ₹2,400." },
      { id: 9, difficulty: "Medium", question: "The correcting entry for the partial omission above (with the difference parked in Suspense) is:", options: ["Suspense A/c Dr. ₹2,400; To Sales A/c ₹2,400", "Sales A/c Dr. ₹2,400; To Suspense A/c ₹2,400", "Kiran's A/c Dr. ₹2,400; To Sales A/c ₹2,400", "Cash A/c Dr. ₹2,400; To Sales A/c ₹2,400"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Only Sales needs correcting (the missing credit), so the other side of the entry goes to Suspense A/c — which also clears the ₹2,400 difference." },
      { id: 10, difficulty: "Medium", question: "Which of these does NOT affect the agreement of the Trial Balance?", options: ["An error of principle", "A casting error in one account", "An amount posted to the wrong side", "A credit balance omitted entirely"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "An error of principle keeps equal debits and credits in the books — only the account class is wrong — so the Trial Balance agrees." },
      { id: 11, difficulty: "Medium", question: "Wages A/c was overcast by ₹1,500. The rectifying entry is:", options: ["Suspense A/c Dr. ₹1,500; To Wages A/c ₹1,500", "Wages A/c Dr. ₹1,500; To Suspense A/c ₹1,500", "Wages A/c Dr. ₹1,500; To Cash A/c ₹1,500", "Suspense A/c Dr. ₹3,000; To Wages A/c ₹3,000"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "A debit-balance account overcast by ₹1,500 has ₹1,500 too much in debits — credit it to remove the excess, and debit Suspense (which had been credited to force agreement)." },
      { id: 12, difficulty: "Medium", question: "Two errors of ₹800 each, one on each side of the Trial Balance, cancel out. These are:", options: ["Compensating errors", "Errors of omission", "Errors of principle", "Suspense errors"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Independent errors of equal value that neutralise each other's effect on the totals are called compensating errors." },
      { id: 13, difficulty: "Medium", question: "If the Suspense A/c still shows a balance after rectifications, it means:", options: ["Some one-sided error is yet to be located", "The rectifications were all correct", "The final accounts can now be relied upon", "The Trial Balance now disagrees"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "The Suspense A/c absorbs exactly the unexplained difference — any leftover balance means part of that difference is still unexplained." },
      { id: 14, difficulty: "Hard", question: "Salary of ₹6,000 was credited to Salaries A/c instead of debited. The Trial Balance difference is:", options: ["₹12,000 — the debit side is short by double the amount", "₹6,000 — the debit side is short", "₹6,000 — the credit side is short", "Nil — it self-corrects"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Replacing a ₹6,000 debit with a ₹6,000 credit shifts the totals by ₹12,000 — ₹6,000 missing from debits plus ₹6,000 wrongly on credits." },
      { id: 15, difficulty: "Hard", question: "The rectifying entry for that wrong-side salary posting is:", options: ["Salaries A/c Dr. ₹12,000; To Suspense A/c ₹12,000", "Salaries A/c Dr. ₹6,000; To Suspense A/c ₹6,000", "Suspense A/c Dr. ₹12,000; To Salaries A/c ₹12,000", "Salaries A/c Dr. ₹6,000; To Cash A/c ₹6,000"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "₹12,000 debited to Salaries removes the wrong credit (₹6,000) and records the right debit (₹6,000); Suspense absorbs the other side and closes." },
      { id: 16, difficulty: "Hard", question: "Goods worth ₹1,000 taken by the owner for personal use were credited to Drawings instead of debited. The Trial Balance:", options: ["Disagrees — the entry is entirely wrong on both counts", "Agrees — Drawings was involved either way", "Agrees — the amount is the same", "Disagrees by ₹2,000"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Drawings should have been DEBITED (owner's drawings reduce capital) — crediting it means the debit side lost ₹1,000 and the credit side gained ₹1,000, so the Trial Balance is out by ₹2,000." },
      { id: 17, difficulty: "Hard", question: "A Trial Balance is short on the debit side by ₹3,500. While rectifying, the accountant finds only one account (a creditor) needs correction. The entry is:", options: ["Suspense A/c Dr. ₹3,500; To Creditor's A/c ₹3,500 — one-sided, via Suspense", "Creditor's A/c Dr. ₹3,500; To Cash A/c ₹3,500", "Bank A/c Dr. ₹3,500; To Creditor's A/c ₹3,500", "No entry is possible with one account only"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Whenever a rectification touches only ONE account, the balancing side must be the Suspense A/c — that is the rule that makes one-sided corrections possible." },
      { id: 18, difficulty: "Hard", question: "Which combination makes an agreeing Trial Balance hide the most damage?", options: ["An error of principle plus a compensating pair — all invisible to the totals", "A casting error only", "A wrong amount only", "A partial omission only"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Errors of principle and compensating errors never disturb the totals — the Trial Balance gives no signal, and only a careful checking of the accounts reveals them." },
      { id: 19, difficulty: "Medium", question: "Commission received ₹700 was posted to the debit side of Commission Received A/c. The correcting entry (through Suspense) is:", options: ["Commission Received A/c Dr. ₹1,400; To Suspense A/c ₹1,400", "Commission Received A/c Dr. ₹700; To Suspense A/c ₹700", "Suspense A/c Dr. ₹1,400; To Commission A/c ₹1,400", "Cash A/c Dr. ₹700; To Commission A/c ₹700"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "A wrong-side posting needs double the amount on the correct side: ₹700 removes the wrong debit and ₹700 records the credit that was missing — ₹1,400 in one entry." },
      { id: 20, difficulty: "Easy", question: "Detection of errors is important mainly because:", options: ["It ensures the final accounts show the true profit and financial position", "It increases the Trial Balance totals", "It avoids the need for a Suspense A/c", "It is required by the auditor personally"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Every error distorts either profit or the Balance Sheet (often both) — finding them is what makes the final accounts trustworthy." },
      { id: 21, difficulty: "Medium", question: "The difference in a Trial Balance when the debit total is ₹87,300 and the credit total is ₹83,700 is:", options: ["₹3,600 — debit side heavier, Suspense A/c credited with ₹3,600", "₹3,600 — credit side heavier, Suspense A/c debited with ₹3,600", "₹1,800 — half the difference", "₹87,300 — the full debit total"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "Difference = ₹87,300 − ₹83,700 = ₹3,600. Since credits are short, Suspense is CREDITED ₹3,600 to force agreement while the error is traced." },
      { id: 22, difficulty: "Hard", question: "Why must each compensating error be rectified separately, even though the Trial Balance agrees?", options: ["Each account must show its true figure — the totals being right does not make the accounts right", "Because the Suspense A/c requires it", "Because the Trial Balance will otherwise disagree", "Compensating errors do not actually need rectifying"], answerIndex: 0, explanation: "The cancellation happens only in the totals. Each affected ledger account still carries a wrong balance, so the Balance Sheet and P&L built from them would be wrong — each error needs its own correcting entry." }
    ]
