      "answer": "The mistake is assuming that agreement of the Trial Balance proves accuracy. The Trial Balance only proves that total debits equal total credits — it cannot detect errors that affect both sides equally. Two examples: (1) an error of principle, such as charging the purchase of machinery to Repairs A/c, which distorts both the asset and the expense; (2) a compensating error, where a ₹500 debit mistake in one account is offset by a ₹500 credit mistake in another. Both leave the Trial Balance agreeing while the financial statements remain wrong — which is why the ledger, subsidiary books and supporting records must also be verified."
